OpenTyrian
================================================================================

OpenTyrian is an open-source port of the DOS game Tyrian.

Tyrian is an arcade-style vertical scrolling shooter.  The story is set
in 20,031 where you play as Trent Hawkins, a skilled fighter-pilot employed
to fight MicroSol and save the galaxy.

Tyrian features a story mode, one- and two-player arcade modes, and networked
multiplayer.

== Additional Necessary Files ==================================================

Tyrian 2.1 data files which have been released as freeware:
  https://camanis.net/tyrian/tyrian21.zip

Place the original data files in data under the game's working directory
(visualc\data when running from Visual Studio).

Run the Data Extractor first to build the eight named .tyrr category files
listed below. OpenTyrian requires these files and the original Tyrian assets.

Both data and visualc\data are ignored by Git; neither the original assets
nor the generated definitions are committed.

== Standalone Data Extractor ===================================================

The Visual Studio solution contains a data_extractor console project.
Build it with the same configuration and platform as the game, then run it
from visualc to export category files for the game:

  cd visualc
  .\data_extractor-x64-Debug.exe --data .\data

Use --data to point to the directory containing the original Tyrian 2.1
assets (in particular tyrian.hdt). Output always goes into data beneath the
current working directory. The executable creates eight text files:

  data\enemy\enemies.tyrr
  data\shots\shots.tyrr
  data\items\ships.tyrr
  data\items\weapons.tyrr
  data\items\generators.tyrr
  data\items\shields.tyrr
  data\items\sidekicks.tyrr
  data\items\specials.tyrr

Each record starts with a [type:id] separator (for example [ship:1]),
followed by key=value fields, including id, and a blank line before the next
record. Existing category files are preserved as a whole, including local
edits. To extract a fresh set without changing local edits, run it from a
separate empty directory with --data pointing to the original asset directory.

The extractor exports item, enemy and shot definitions without starting
the game, graphics or audio.

== Modding and Load Order ======================================================

OpenTyrian explicitly loads the named files in this order:
shots.tyrr, ships.tyrr, weapons.tyrr, generators.tyrr, shields.tyrr,
sidekicks.tyrr, specials.tyrr, enemies.tyrr (at the paths listed above).
These eight files are required, even when mods supply replacement objects.

Next it loads other .tyrr files directly in data\items, data\enemy and
data\shots, globally sorted by relative path, alphabetically ignoring case
(case-sensitive order breaks ties). It does not search data itself or any
nested subfolders. The eight named files are not loaded a second time.

Any .tyrr file can contain a mixture of the supported object types:
ship, weapon, generator, shield, sidekick, special, shot and enemy.
For example, data\items\addon.tyrr may contain:

  [generator:2]
  name=Mod Generator
  power=5

  [ship:1]
  name=Mod Ship
  shipgraphic=233

  [weapon:1]
  name=Mod Weapon
  op0=customshot

  [shot:customshot]
  multi=1
  max=1

Objects are identified by type AND ID. A later record with the same type
and ID replaces the entire earlier object; omitted fields default to zero,
not to the earlier object's values. The last record wins even within a
single file. New IDs add objects without changing existing objects' slots.
References are resolved only after all files are loaded, allowing references
to objects defined later in the same bundle or in a later file.

Headers supply IDs; an optional id= field must match its header.
Each type must retain its reserved ID 0 object, which always occupies slot zero
regardless of record order.
IDs are case-sensitive, contain 1-10 non-whitespace characters, and cannot
contain : [ ] = # or ;. Blank lines and full-line # or ; comments are allowed.
Fields use key=value syntax. Parse errors and missing references report the
file and line; missing required files direct you to run Data Extractor.

== Keyboard Controls ===========================================================

alt-enter      -- toggle full-screen

arrow keys     -- ship movement
space          -- fire weapons
enter          -- toggle rear weapon mode
ctrl/alt       -- fire left/right sidekick

== Network Multiplayer =========================================================

Currently OpenTyrian does not have an arena; as such, networked games must be
initiated manually via the command line simultaneously by both players.

syntax:
  opentyrian --net HOSTNAME --net-player-name NAME --net-player-number NUMBER

where HOSTNAME is the IP address of your opponent, NUMBER is either 1 or 2
depending on which ship you intend to pilot, and NAME is your alias

OpenTyrian uses UDP port 1333 for multiplayer, but in most cases players will
not need to open any ports because OpenTyrian makes use of UDP hole punching.

